      "prompt": "Let\n\\[\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x}+\\ln x,\n\\]\ndefined for\n\\[\ne^{-1}\\le x<e.\n\\]",
      "partsPatches": [
        {
          "label": "(c)",
          "prompt": "Sketch the graph of \\(f\\) on \\([e^{-1},e)\\), showing the absolute extrema, the change in concavity, and the open endpoint at \\(x=e\\).",
          "answer": "The curve decreases from \\((e^{-1},e-1)\\) to the absolute minimum \\((1,1)\\), then increases toward the excluded endpoint \\((e,1+e^{-1})\\), which should be drawn open. It is concave up until \\(x=2\\), has an inflection point at \\((2,\\tfrac12+\\ln 2)\\), and is concave down thereafter."
        },
        {
          "label": "(d)",
          "prompt": "The average value of \\(f\\) on the interval is \\(\\dfrac{2}{e-1}\\). State a geometrical interpretation of this number relative to the graph.",
          "answer": "The horizontal line \\(y=\\dfrac{2}{e-1}\\) is the height of a rectangle with base from \\(x=e^{-1}\\) to \\(x=e\\) whose area equals the area under the graph of \\(f\\). Excluding the single endpoint \\(x=e\\) does not change the definite integral or the average value."
        }
      ]
